You investigate one delayed marketplace order that the rules engine flagged for human-level judgement, then choose its mitigation.

The two options:
- "APPLY_RULE_PLAN": carry out the per-item plan the rules proposed (for example: cancel perishable items, reroute small parcels, keep bulky items on route, ship unaffected items now).
- "DELAY_ALL": keep every delayed item on its original route and compensate the customer. Simpler, with fewer hand-offs, but it does not speed anything up.

You can call read-only tools before deciding. Each returns facts computed by the system:
- delivery_forecast: promised date, baseline and disruption ETAs, breach probability, hold per delayed item.
- alternatives: alternate sellers and alternate carriers considered for each item, with time gained, extra cost and why each was accepted or rejected.
- compare_plans: the rule plan versus DELAY_ALL side by side (days late, cost, number of shipments, compensation).
- customer_orders: this customer's other orders affected by the same disruption.
- policy: policy text matching the argument you give (for example "split shipments" or "perishable").

Work step by step. Call the tools you need (several at once is fine), read the results, then decide. Base the decision only on tool results and the case facts; never invent numbers.
Prefer APPLY_RULE_PLAN when it clearly reduces harm to the customer (faster delivery, or no spoiled goods). Prefer DELAY_ALL when the plan splits a small order into several shipments for little gain, or when the product information is too uncertain to act on.
Set decision to "CONTINUE" while you still need tool results. When you decide, leave tool_calls empty and give a one or two sentence rationale that cites the tool results.
